cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Captured weight and boundary leakage bound the error of sample-based spectral functions"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
abstract: >-
  Sample-based quantum diagonalization returns a subspace of measured configurations; Rayleigh-Ritz on
  it yields a spectral function, and we ask what bounds its error. The captured Born weight w alone
  does not: Rayleigh-Ritz displaces the poles it keeps, an analytic counterexample forces any bound
  indexed on it to its trivial value, and on the probe's support, where w=1, the error is still
  0.35-0.43 at η=0.18t against a ceiling of 2. What bounds it, with the missed weight, is the
  Hamiltonian coupling across the subspace boundary: for Rayleigh-Ritz on any orthogonal projector,
  given the exact ground state, we prove that the relative L_1 error lies between 1-w and
  min{1+w,(1+sqrt(w))(sqrt(1-w)+Λ(η)/η)}, with Λ the boundary coupling at resolution η, from the Ritz
  pairs and the retained probe. For the 1D Hubbard A(ω), built from classical Born draws, or their
  infinite-shot ranking, of a real-time evolved probe (one device run, an execution record, aside),
  the bound first beats the trivial one at a true error of 1.2-7.3×10^-3 in all sixteen (L,η) cells
  over L=6-12; a two-constant rule in 1-w, fitted here without guarantee, is tighter on 11 of 14
  splits; the Born-ranked A(k,ω) at 85% of its sector is bounded at 350-900 times its true error. Yet
  at all five sizes up to dimension 10306296 the bound is vacuous at every operating fraction of the
  resource scan, by 1.66-8.61, and informative only well above them. In the determinant basis the
  probe's Krylov space has, at L=6-14, coordinate support on every symmetry-allowed determinant, so
  zero leakage at full weight needs all of them; a momentum probe measured in plane-wave orbitals
  stays in one block, 1/L of the sector, a gain of 1/L against an exponential. On pairing-model fibers
  every orbital-invariant leakage bound is vacuous. At full weight a second-order certificate follows;
  below it, where every operating fraction lies, one is open.
